ack\n\n##### Manage consent\n\n##### Details Cookies\n\n##### Cookie policy\n\nOur websites use cookies to offer you a better browsing experience by enabling, optimising, and analysing site operations, as well as to provide personalised ad content and allow you to connect to social media. By choosing “Accept all” you consent to the use of cookies and the related processing of personal data. Select “Manage consent” to manage your consent preferences. You can change your preferences or retract your consent at any time via the cookie policy page. Please view our [cookie policy](https://www.home.saxo/en-mena/legal/cookie-policy/saxo-cookie-policy) and our [privacy policy](https://www.home.saxo/en-mena/legal/privacy-policy/saxo-privacy-policy).\n\n##### Necessary\n\nShow more\n\n##### Preference\n\nYes No\n\nShow more\n\nYes No\n\n##### Statistical\n\nYes No\n\nShow more\n\nYes No\n\n##### Marketing\n\nYes No\n\nShow more\n\nYes No\n\nAccept all Necessary only Manage consent Save and close Accept all\n\nQT_QuickTake\n\n# Market Quick Take - Metals slide while Fed minutes point to further hikes - 19 November 2026\n\n[Macro](https://www.home.saxo/en-mena/insights/news-and-research/macro)   3 minutes to read\n\n![Saxo Be Invested](https://www.home.saxo/-/media/content-hub/images/general/author-profile-pictures/saxo-be-invested-image.png)\n\n##### Saxo Bank\n\nSaxo Bank\n\n- [https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=&lt;url&gt;](https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=%3Curl%3E)\n- [https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?mini=true&amp;url=&lt;url&gt;&amp;title=Market Quick Take - Metals slide while Fed minutes point to further hikes - 19 November 2026](&lt;https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?mini=true&amp;url=%3Curl%3E&amp;title=Market Quick Take - Metals slide while Fed minutes point to further hikes - 19 November 2026&gt;)\n- [https://x.com/intent/tweet?url=&lt;url&gt;&amp;text=Market Quick Take - Metals slide while Fed minutes point to further hikes - 19 November 2026&amp;via=saxobank](&lt;https://x.com/intent/tweet?url=%3Curl%3E&amp;text=Market Quick Take - Metals slide while Fed minutes point to further hikes - 19 November 2026&amp;via=saxobank&gt;)\n\n## **Market drivers and catalysts**\n\n- **Macro**: Big USD rally driving global liquidity concerns?\n- **Equities**: US equities closed mixed as technology outperformed, European markets declined on mining weakness, while Asian equities diverged amid renewed selling in Hong Kong.\n- **Volatility:** Fear eased as one-day hedging fell and futures pointed to calmer markets by December\n- **Digital Assets**: Bitcoin steadied near USD 63,000 while Strategy led crypto shares lower and funds bled\n- **Commodities**: Commodity selloff deepens amid hawkish Fed, growth fears and tighter liquidity\n- **Fixed Income**: Fed minutes hawkish, but front-end treasury yields little changed. Longer treasury yields drop.\n- **Currencies**: USD strength persists, AUD gutted by weak October jobs report.\n\n---\n\n## **Macro**\n\n- **Minutes of the October Fed meeting showed nearly all officials agreed further rate increases would likely be needed** in the months ahead, and Governor Waller said a half-point hike should be considered at the December meeting, citing core inflation running near 3.5% annualised over three months. The market has by now priced in three more increases, taking the policy rate to 4.50% to 4.75% by June 2027.\n- **US housing starts rose 9.9% in October** to an annualised 1.309 million, above the 1.250 million expected, while building permits fell 7.4% to 1.295 million against 1.375 million expected, a reading seen as signalling a weaker pipeline. UK inflation rose to a one-year high of 3.6% in October from 3.5% in September, with transport and motor fuel costs the main contributors.\n- **Geopolitics:** President Trump signalled that Ukraine will feature in today&#x27;s trilateral meeting with Xi Jinping and Vladimir Putin, following an earlier meeting with Putin that reportedly ended on a positive note. Trump also praised his relationship with Xi and downplayed the Nvidia spying controversy. Meanwhile, tensions with Iran remain elevated, with the New York Times reporting that Trump is considering fresh military strikes amid indications Tehran is rebuilding its missile stockpile.\n\nReports of a technical disruption to the NSA&#x27;s network coincided with a safe-haven bid for Treasuries and the yen on Tuesday.\n\nMore in our [**Macro Analysis &amp; Macroeconomic News**](https://www.home.saxo/insights/news-and-research/macro)\n\n#### **Macro calendar highlights (times in GMT)**\n\n- 1330 – US Nov. Philadelphia Fed Business Outlook\n- 1330 – US Weekly Initial Jobless Claims\n- 1600 – US Nov. Kansas City Fed Manufacturing Activity\n- 2330 – Japan Oct. National CPI\n- 0001 – UK Nov. GfK Consumer Confidence\n- Fed speakers: Hammack (1350 - voter), Jefferson (1425 - voter). Goolsbee (1730 - voter in 2027), Barr (1915 - voter) &amp; Barkin (2100 – voter in 2027)\n- Trump, Xi and Putin hold trilateral meeting in Shenzhen\n\n#### **Earnings events**\n\n- **Thursday**: Walmart, Tokio Marine Holdings, Intuit\n\nFor all macro, earnings, and dividend events check Saxo’s [calendar.](https://www.saxotrader.com/d/research/calendar)\n\n---\n\n## **Equities**\n\n- **USA**: The S&amp;P 500 fell 0.2% for a fifth consecutive decline and the Dow lost 1.1%, while the Nasdaq 100 rose 0.4% as technology proved more resilient. Federal Reserve minutes reinforced concerns over further interest-rate increases, while Nvidia dropped 2.9% despite strong earnings and guidance, as investors continued to question its leadership transition. UnitedHealth sank 10.2% after proposed tighter Medicare Advantage audits, while TJX gained 8.1% following strong sales and an improved outlook. Palo Alto Networks added almost 10% during regular trading and rose further after hours following upbeat results, ahead of Anthropic&#x27;s market debut.\n- **Europe**: The Stoxx 600 fell 1% for a third consecutive session, while the DAX lost 1.2%, the FTSE 100 declined 1.3% and the CAC 40 slipped 0.9% as falling commodity prices weighed on sentiment. Mining stocks dropped 4.7% as copper hit a seven-month low, pulling Antofagasta down 7.9%, while Glencore lost more than 5%. SAP fell 3.8% amid renewed pressure on software stocks, while ASML gained 1.9% as Nvidia&#x27;s strong revenue outlook supported chip equipment suppliers. Elsewhere, Alstom dropped 7.2% after reporting a net loss, adding to concerns over corporate earnings.\n- **Asia**: Asian equities traded mixed on Thursday, with Hong Kong’s Hang Seng down 1.9%, Japan’s Nikkei 225 rising 0.7% and South Korea’s Kospi fluctuating, while Australia’s ASX 200 closed down 1%. Hong Kong technology stocks led the retreat as Tencent fell 7% in afternoon trading after disappointing revenue growth and rising artificial intelligence spending raised concerns over profitability, while Alibaba weakened ahead of its earnings. In Australia, BHP dropped 3.7% and Rio Tinto lost 4.1% as copper prices slumped, while Japanese technology stocks helped offset weakness in banks and metals. Investors awaited Alibaba’s results and Japan’s inflation figures for further direction.\n\nMore in our [**Equity Trading - Stock Market Analysis &amp; News**](https://www.home.saxo/insights/news-and-research/equities)\n\n---\n\n## **Volatility**\n\n**VIX 24.56 | VIX FUTURES: 21.400 (Dec) | TERM STRUCTURE: FLAT (cash) | SKEW: ELEVATED (142.84) | MARKET REGIME: NEUTRAL / CHOP | AS OF ~05:00 CET**\n\n- Investors grew a little calmer on Wednesday, but they are not yet treating this week&#x27;s selloff as over. The VIX eased to 24.56 after jumping about seven points in a week, and **demand for one-day protection fell sharply**. Futures tell a more reassuring story: **traders expect the fear to fade by December**, with later months priced below today&#x27;s level and barely rising beyond that.\n- The 9 December Fed meeting, where **a half-point hike is now openly discussed**, is the next big test. Options price a move of about 1.12% for today, with Walmart and Alibaba results ahead. Volatility in the bond market also eased, backing the calmer tone.\n\nMore in our [**Options Trading - Stock Market Analysis &amp; News**](https://www.home.saxo/insights/news-and-research/options)\n\n---\n\n## **Digital Assets**\n\n**BITCOIN ~63,069 +0.11% | ETHEREUM ~1,787 +0.67% | IBIT 35.36 -2.16% | ETHA 39.58 -2.63% | AS OF ~05:00 CET**\n\n- Bitcoin steadied near **USD 63,000** after losing about 12% in a week. Traders being forced out of leveraged bets made the drop worse. Shares in crypto companies fell harder than the coins. **Strategy lost 6.40%** as investors questioned its habit of selling new shares to buy back its preferred stock, with bitcoin now well below the average price the company paid. Riot went the other way, rising almost 7%.\n- **Bitcoin funds lost about USD 306m** on Wednesday, almost half of it from BlackRock&#x27;s IBIT. Wells Fargo&#x27;s filing, though, showed it more than doubled its IBIT holding last quarter. Sentiment sits at extreme fear.\n\n---\n\n## **Commodities**\n\n- **Copper slumped 5% yesterday to a seven-month low**, pressured by global growth concerns, disappointing Chinese demand signals and expectations of increased US exports. Negative sentiment at a major industry conference in Shanghai triggered heavy speculative selling, while reports that more than 100,000 tonnes of copper could be redirected to China in December eased concerns about tight local supplies. Despite the sharp selloff, the longer-term outlook remains supported by tight scrap and mine supplies, resilient infrastructure demand and committed AI-related investment.\n- **Precious metals**: Gold extended its recent slump, touching a fresh 15-month low of USD 3,694 in Asia, as hawkish Fed minutes and Governor Waller&#x27;s comments reinforced expectations of further rate hikes. Beyond higher yields and rate hike concerns, the broader commodity selloff may also reflect tightening global dollar liquidity as the dollar continues to rally. Rising AI-related financing needs, IPO activity, equity market volatility and elevated energy security costs are increasing demand for cash just as higher interest rates make funding more expensive. Gold, given its deep liquidity, and other metals may therefore be caught in a broader *dash for cash*, with investors selling liquid assets to meet funding needs and reduce risk exposure.\n- **Oil**: Brent trades near unchanged around USD 92 after falling 1.3% on the Wednesday. Alongside broader risk reduction across commodities, recent weakness has been driven by recovering Middle East production and exports, renewing concerns about an emerging supply glut. However, the risk of fresh disruptions remains, with the New York Times reporting that Trump, under renewed pressure from Israel&#x27;s Netanyahu, is considering fresh strikes against Iran. Tehran has responded by warning neighbouring countries of retaliation should they allow US attacks to be launched from their territory.\n\nMore in our [**Commodity News, Analysis &amp; Commentary**](https://www.home.saxo/insights/news-and-research/commodities)\n\n---\n\n## **Fixed Income**\n\n- **US Treasury yields edged a bit lower at the long end of the curve, while short-date yields remained rangebound.** The benchmark 2-year treasury yield traded little changed near 4.80% While the benchmark 10-year treasury yield moved four basis points lower Wednesday to close near 5.07% and was even down close to 5.05% in early Thursday trading. A US Treasury auction of 20-year notes saw demand in the middle of the range of prior auctions, which was better than the demand metrics at recent 10-year and 30-year treasury auctions.\n- **Japan’s 20-year JGB auction:** Demand was notably weak, with a bid-to-cover ratio of 3.64 versus 5.84 at the prior auction and a 12-month average of 3.92, reflecting reduced appetite for long-duration amid rising inflation and rate expectations.\n\n---\n\n## **Currencies**\n\n- **The US dollar remains strong, pulling to new highs for the cycle against a weak AUD** (more on that below) and CAD, while elsewhere price action was muted. **EURUSD** probed almost to 1.0950 in a new low since early 2025 Wednesday, but rebounded slightly by early Thursday. **GBPUSD** was slightly weaker than EURUSD on relative GBP softness and extended its range beyond the 2026 lows of 1.3136, where it trades early Thursday. The JPY’s relative strength faded overnight into early Thursday as **USDJPY** rebounded above 159.50 after a low of 159.13 Wednesday,\n- **AUD was hit by a weak October jobs report and the spike in the unemployment rate to 4.9%**, sending AUD to the lowest levels since the first week of 2026 below 0.6665. AUDNZD fell to a new local low below 1.2140 before rebounding slightly. AUD sentiment has also been hit by the weakness in copper prices.\n\nMore on currencies in our dedicated section: [**Forex Trading News &amp; Analysis**](https://www.home.saxo/insights/news-and-research/forex)\n\n##### Share\n\n- [https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=&lt;url&gt;](https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=%3Curl%3E)\n- [https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?mini=true&amp;url=&lt;url&gt;&amp;title=Market Quick Take - Metals slide while Fed minutes point to further hikes - 19 November 2026](&lt;https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?mini=true&amp;url=%3Curl%3E&amp;title=Market Quick Take - Metals slide while Fed minutes point to further hikes - 19 November 2026&gt;)\n- [https://x.com/intent/tweet?url=&lt;url&gt;&amp;text=Market Quick Take - Metals slide while Fed minutes point to further hikes - 19 November 2026&amp;via=saxobank](&lt;https://x.com/intent/tweet?url=%3Curl%3E&amp;text=Market Quick Take - Metals slide while Fed minutes point to further hikes - 19 November 2026&amp;via=saxobank&gt;)\n\n- [https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=&lt;url&gt;](https://www.facebook.com/share.php?u=%3Curl%3